Ladybird now runs the HTML parser for a document whose body arrives
after it’s activated with an empty JavaScript execution context stack,
so the parser’s microtask checkpoint before each script it prepares,
and the one in “clean up after running script”, both run. Blink
checks for microtasks there whenever no script is executing, in
HTMLParserScriptRunner::ProcessScriptElementInternal(); WebKit does in
HTMLScriptRunner::runScript(). And Gecko holds an nsAutoMicroTask in
ScriptLoader::ProcessRequest(), which runs them when it goes away.

Run in Chrome 157.0.8103.79, Firefox 151.0, and WebKit 26.5, a reduction
of Cookiebot’s blocking mode lets a mutation observer change the second
of two parser-inserted scripts to text/plain before it runs. And in all
three, a promise reaction queued by an XMLHttpRequest readystatechange
listener runs before the request’s progress listener does.
